Done, with one caveat that comes from the next point. SMTP rejects
recipients one at a time, at RCPT TO. Whether the rest of the message
still goes out is the client's choice. msmtp sends no DATA if any
recipient is refused, and exits non-zero with the server's reply. I
checked this in its source (smtp_send_envelope). The spec makes msmtp
the sender in lab-claude, and the acceptance test checks that a typo
recipient means nobody gets the message.

> I'm okay with doing the rejection in OpenSMTPD config. Though I suggest
> more research to see if there's a setting to allow rejections to pass
> through, i.e. to not accept immediately but rather to proxy. Fine if
> not, I just want to be certain that's not an option.

It is an option, and I changed the spec to use it. I found:

- OpenSMTPD always queues. smtpd.conf(5) has no mode that relays
  before it answers.
- Postfix has smtpd_proxy_filter, which holds the session open while
  it talks to another SMTP server. That server is meant to be a local
  filter that hands mail back to Postfix. It cannot log in to Fastmail
  itself, so it does not solve this.
- smtprelay (mail/smtprelay, maintained by decke@) is built for this.
  It relays each message to the upstream server inside the client's
  DATA command. If upstream answers with an SMTP error, smtprelay
  returns that code and text to the client; for other failures, such
  as Fastmail being unreachable, it returns 421. It also has the two
  checks we need: allowed_sender and allowed_recipients (regular
  expressions).

With smtprelay, lab-mail never accepts a message it has not handed to
Fastmail and never sends a bounce, so there is no queue to watch.

The cost is the whole-message rule. OpenSMTPD could enforce that on
the server with a builtin filter that disconnects at the first bad
RCPT TO. smtprelay cannot, so the rule rests on msmtp as above. I think
pass-through is worth more, because it is the only way the agent sees
Fastmail's own reasons. Do you agree, or would you rather keep
OpenSMTPD?

> I'm confused, I thought you said we need to check the sender, because
