> The agent needs to know if its send was rejected, and why.

You are right, and v1 contradicted itself. It turned your "don't
rewrite" into "don't check", and then it relied on a bounce the agent
might never see.

v2 covers both cases. lab-mail checks the sender and the recipients
itself, so those mistakes are rejected in the session with a clear
reply. Anything Fastmail rejects comes back in the same session,
through smtprelay. In both cases msmtp exits non-zero and prints the
reply, so the agent knows that it failed and why.

The one case left is a failure after Fastmail has accepted the message,
e.g. the remote side of pat@patmaddox.com refusing it later. Fastmail
then bounces to claude@p7x.dev. All the allowed recipients are at your
own domains, so this should be rare, and the spec lists it as out of
scope.

Three questions remain, and each would change v2:

